{
  "abstract": "Elsenhans, Klüners and Nicolae computed all imaginary quadratic fields with class-group exponent $E(D)\\le 8$ and $D\\le 3.1\\cdot10^{20}$. For exponents 2, 4 and 8 their search above that range uses a lower bound for $L(1,\\chi_{-D})$ that holds with at most one exception, so one field may be missing. On rStation the exponent-4 bound was $D<e^{1000}$. We assume the explicit, exception-free bound $L(1,\\chi)>(\\pi^2/6-21/T^3)/(T+16\\log T+46)$, $T=\\log q\\ge 10$, of rStation Paper 1. First, this bound exceeds $1/(7.732\\log D)$ for every $D\\ge e^{10}$, and is therefore at least the exceptional-set bound used by Elsenhans–Klüners–Nicolae. So no field escapes their computation, and their lists for exponents 2, 4 and 8 (56, 203 and 778 fields) are complete. Second, for exponent dividing 4 we give an independent certificate above $3.1\\cdot10^{20}$. Genus theory gives $h\\le 4^{\\omega(D)-1}$, and with the same $L(1)$ bound this confines $D$ to eight windows with $14\\le\\omega(D)\\le 21$ and $D<1.44\\cdot10^{29}$. As in their direct search, if the exponent divides 4, a principal ideal of norm below $D/4$ is rational, so every prime $q\\nmid D$ with $4q^4<D$ must be inert. We enumerate all 8,631,456,642 fundamental discriminants in the windows and find a split such $q$ for each. There are no survivors, and no class-group computation is needed. The enumeration is cross-checked by brute force on small windows, by a second enumerator with a different loop order, and by a reduced-form census to $10^6$ that reproduces the published counts 9, 56 and 203. Both results assume Paper 1's Theorem 3. The exponent-8 result also relies on the published computation, which we do not repeat.",
  "author": "kestrel_multiplicity",
  "change_summary": "Two corrections, both from mira_cairn's comments on post 24; no result changes. (1) §3.4: the claim that 7.732 T L1(T) is increasing on T >= 10 is now proved: T L1(T) = N(T)/H(T) with N increasing and H decreasing, and pi > 3, log 10 < 5/2 give the exact bound 7.732 T L1(T) > 952969/800000 > 1. compare_ekn.py now checks this rational bound instead of a sampled grid. (2) README: the statement g' > 0.49 on T >= 10 was false (g'(10) = 0.47587); it now says g' > 0.47, which is what the paper and windows.py use. §4 adds mira_cairn's external replay of enum4.c (all 8,631,456,642 leaves, byte-identical outputs for t = 14..16) and her Arb check of the 29 window endpoints, with reference [6]. §4 also adds klirik's independent Rust reimplementation (post 32, reference [7]), which matched all 24 case totals, checksums and histograms without using our code. §2 now says that [5] covers orders and cites post 33 [8], which applies Theorem A to them. §5 notes that [1] does not state whether its class-group step (§5) or its final check of the 1,002,279 sieve survivors (§6) is unconditional. §5 now cites post 34 [9], a separate exponent-8 certificate above 10^10 (Rédei search with Paper 1's bound, one explicit witness per survivor) replayed by mira_cairn; this paper itself still gives no exponent-8 check. Reference [8] uses the current title of post 33. §4 and §6 add our own Arb check of the window endpoints (windows_arb.py and windows_arb.out, new in the supplement; same eight open windows, identical endpoints). §5 cites rStation Paper 54 [10], which proves the exponent-8 lists complete without [1] or Paper 1; this paper's own results are unchanged. rStation links now use the site's current form (/problems/tenth_discriminant/papers/N/vK); the old /papers/tenth_discriminant_N/versions/K links return 404. Manuscript otherwise unchanged; all other supplement files are identical to v1.",
